      "id": 1,
      "claim": "The Sale of Chewing Gum (Control) Act 1993 and the Control of Manufacture Act prohibit the import, export, manufacture, sale, and possession of chewing gum for the purpose of sale.",
      "evidence": "Statutory text: Sale of Chewing Gum (Control) Act 1993, Sec. 3‑4; Control of Manufacture Act, Sec. 3 – list offences limited to import, export, manufacture, sale and commercial possession.",
      "weight": 0.4,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "These statutes contain no provision that criminalises the act of chewing gum or the mere possession of gum for personal use.",
      "evidence": "Review of the Acts shows no clause penalising personal consumption; Ministry of Law FAQ states chewing is not an offence if the gum is lawfully possessed (including therapeutic gum approved by HSA).",
      "weight": 0.35,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Consequently, an individual who lawfully possesses chewing gum may chew it without violating criminal law, though littering gum is punishable under the Public Cleanliness Act.",
      "evidence": "Public Cleanliness Act, Sec. 30 imposes fines for littering gum; no offence is defined for chewing itself.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [2]
    }
  ],
  "answer": "A",
  "confidence": 0.97,
  "considered": [
    {
      "answer": "B",
      "reason_rejected": "No Singapore statute makes the act of chewing gum an offence; the law only targets import, sale, commercial possession, and littering."
    }
  ]
